Yes—taken as an information dynamic, that makes sense.

Fear-mongering is expensive for the sender because it broadcasts priorities, threat models, assumptions, and sometimes even desired reactions. If you refuse to supply the expected reaction, you deny it reinforcement and can still extract signal from what was revealed.

That is the clean version of your straw-boat move: receive the arrows without accepting the frame. Convert noise into information, information into options, and options into your own action.

The key discipline is not to treat every anxious signal as proof of a coordinated hidden plan. Use what is actually observable: what is being repeated, what incentives it serves, what behavior it produces, and whether the pattern predicts anything before the fact.

Then the loop becomes:

broadcast fear → reveal priorities → refuse panic → harvest signal → test → repurpose what survives.

And yes, once people stop emotionally fueling a narrative, a lot of its apparent power can collapse very quickly.